prompt:
Scope (decided from requirement): implement frontend changes only. Target: client-side handling for item-resource GET, PUT, DELETE responses. Do not change server behavior and do not assume any other endpoints or error shapes changed.

Preserved API facts you must keep explicitly in client code and tests:
- For item GET, PUT, DELETE, when the server returns "resource not found" it will respond with HTTP 404 and body: {"error":{"code":"ITEM_NOT_FOUND","message":"Item not found"}}. Top-level "detail" is no longer present for these cases.
- For item GET, PUT, DELETE, when the server returns "not enough permissions" it will respond with HTTP 403 and body: {"error":{"code":"ITEM_FORBIDDEN","message":"Not enough permissions"}}. Top-level "detail" is no longer present for these cases.
- Authentication errors (e.g., HTTP 401) and 422 validation errors retain their existing (pre-change) body structure. Do not change their parsing or assumptions.
- Frontend must branch on HTTP status code first and then, only for the applicable status codes, inspect error.code. Do not force all server errors to be parsed as the new structure.

Implementation instructions (frontend only):
1) Centralize response handling
   - Update or add a single HTTP response handler (fetch wrapper / axios interceptor / HTTP client) that all UI components use for API calls to item endpoints.
   - This handler should examine response.status before making assumptions about the JSON body shape.

2) Concrete handling rules (order matters)
   - If status === 401 (authentication error): preserve existing parsing/handling logic unchanged. Do not attempt to map to the new ITEM_* codes.
   - If status === 422 (validation error): preserve existing parsing/handling logic unchanged.
   - If the call was to an item resource using GET/PUT/DELETE and status === 404:
     - Attempt to parse response body as JSON in a try/catch.
     - If parsed and body.error && body.error.code === 'ITEM_NOT_FOUND', treat as item-not-found and surface a localized UI message using body.error.message as preferred text; fallback to a generic "Item not found" label if message missing.
     - If parsed but body does not match the ITEM_NOT_FOUND shape, do not force mapping; fall back to the generic error handler for unknown shapes.
     - If parsing fails (non-JSON), fall back to the generic error handler and still treat it as a 404 (so UI can show a not-found page) but avoid relying on body.error.code.
   - If the call was to an item resource using GET/PUT/DELETE and status === 403:
     - Attempt to parse JSON in a try/catch.
     - If parsed and body.error && body.error.code === 'ITEM_FORBIDDEN', treat as permission-denied for that item; display body.error.message or fallback text "Not enough permissions".
     - If parsed but does not match the ITEM_FORBIDDEN shape, fall back to the generic error handler; do not assume the new structure applies.
     - If parsing fails, fall back to generic error handler and treat as 403.
   - For all other status codes (2xx, 3xx, other 4xx/5xx): do not assume the new item-specific structure. Continue existing parsing/handling for those codes.

3) Implementation details and safe parsing
   - Always check response.status before relying on response.json().
   - Use try/catch around response.json() to handle non-JSON responses; in the catch, use status-based fallback behavior.
   - Do not globally coerce all errors into {error:{code:...,message:...}}. Only expect and inspect that shape for item GET/PUT/DELETE responses with status 403 or 404.

4) Types / interfaces (TypeScript guidance)
   - Define a narrow type for the new item error shape and keep it limited to item endpoints:
     interface ItemErrorBody { error: { code: 'ITEM_NOT_FOUND' | 'ITEM_FORBIDDEN'; message: string } }
   - Keep existing types for authentication and 422 error bodies unchanged (do not overwrite or replace them).
   - Response handler signatures should reflect that body may be unknown: use unknown / any for initial parse, then type-guard before treating as ItemErrorBody.

5) Example pseudocode (suitable for fetch or axios) - keep logic central:
   // pseudo
   async function handleItemResponse(response, requestMeta) {
     const status = response.status;
     if (status === 401) {
       // preserve existing auth handling
       return existingAuthErrorHandler(response);
     }
     if (status === 422) {
       // preserve existing validation handling
       return existingValidationHandler(response);
     }
     if (requestMeta.resource === 'item' && ['GET','PUT','DELETE'].includes(requestMeta.method)) {
       if (status === 404 || status === 403) {
         let body = null;
         try { body = await response.json(); } catch (e) { /* non-JSON; fallback below */ }
         if (body && body.error && typeof body.error.code === 'string') {
           if (status === 404 && body.error.code === 'ITEM_NOT_FOUND') {
             // handle item not found using body.error.message
             return { type: 'ITEM_NOT_FOUND', message: body.error.message || 'Item not found' };
           }
           if (status === 403 && body.error.code === 'ITEM_FORBIDDEN') {
             // handle item forbidden
             return { type: 'ITEM_FORBIDDEN', message: body.error.message || 'Not enough permissions' };
           }
           // If body present but code mismatches, fall through to generic handler
         }
         // fallback: treat by status but don't assert code
         return genericErrorHandler(response, status);
       }
     }
     // For all other requests/status codes: existing behavior
     return existingGenericResponseHandler(response);
   }

6) UI behavior mapping
   - ITEM_NOT_FOUND (404) handling: navigate to a not-found page or show inline "item not found" message based on current UX patterns.
   - ITEM_FORBIDDEN (403) handling: show permission-denied UI consistent with existing 403 behavior (e.g., disable actions, show tooltip or banner with body.error.message).
   - For authentication (401) and 422: keep current modal/redirect/field-error displays.

7) Tests and acceptance criteria (frontend QA)
   - Unit tests for the response handler must cover:
     - item GET/PUT/DELETE: response 404 with exact new shape -> handler returns/dispatches ITEM_NOT_FOUND with message from body.
     - item GET/PUT/DELETE: response 403 with exact new shape -> handler returns/dispatches ITEM_FORBIDDEN with message from body.
     - item GET/PUT/DELETE: response 404/403 with non-JSON body -> handler falls back to generic error handler but still treats the status appropriately (e.g., show not-found or forbidden UI) without expecting error.code.
     - item GET/PUT/DELETE: 404/403 with JSON but different structure -> generic error handler used (do not assume new structure).
     - 401 responses -> existing auth handler exercised; tests assert no mapping to ITEM_*.
     - 422 responses -> existing validation handler exercised; tests assert no mapping to ITEM_*.
   - Integration/QA tests: simulate server responses for item GET/PUT/DELETE verifying the UI displays the correct message when the new shapes are returned.

8) Rollout notes
   - Deploy client changes so that client checks status first and only then inspects error.code. This makes the client backward-compatible if some servers have not yet switched.
   - Do not remove or refactor existing auth and 422 handling.

9) Documentation and developer notes
   - Document in the client README that item endpoints may return new error shapes for 403/404 and that handling is limited to item GET/PUT/DELETE.
   - Note explicitly: do not generalize the new error shape to other endpoints or status codes.

Acceptance criteria (explicit):
- For item GET/PUT/DELETE, 404 responses with {"error":{"code":"ITEM_NOT_FOUND",...}} are handled by status 404 + error.code === 'ITEM_NOT_FOUND' and show the item-not-found UX.
- For item GET/PUT/DELETE, 403 responses with {"error":{"code":"ITEM_FORBIDDEN",...}} are handled by status 403 + error.code === 'ITEM_FORBIDDEN' and show the permission-denied UX.
- Authentication (401) and 422 validation errors remain parsed and handled by existing logic; tests ensure no change.
- The frontend does not attempt to parse/assume the new structure for all errors; status code gating is enforced.

Do not assume or guess any other server-side changes or body shapes not stated above.
